Scientific Breakthrough Propagation

A Mathematical Framework for Estimating the Downstream Impact of AI-Generated Discoveries

Fourth Research Draft — v0.4

Author: Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 


Research collaboration: Human–LLM scientific research

Status: Mathematical specification with corrected proofs and algorithms
Primary case study: The 2026 AI-generated mathematical corpus reported as 722 manuscripts organized into 372 families of results


Abstract

This work provides a corrected mathematical specification of the Scientific Breakthrough Propagation framework. Version 0.4 resolves four critical defects identified in v0.3 and incorporates validated methodological corrections from the current literature.

First, the formal fertility definition is corrected from F = H(𝒢|dᵢ) − H(𝒢), which yields negative values for fertile theorems, to F = H(𝒢) − H(𝒢|dᵢ), the information gain over the proof-search state space. The proof of non-negativity is reconstructed using majorization theory and the Schur-concavity of Shannon entropy, replacing the invalid "mean-preserving contraction" argument.

Second, the logistic activation σ(P) ∈ [½, 1) is replaced by a zero-anchored logarithmic multiplier 1 + ln(1 + P), which equals 1.0 at baseline and scales unboundedly, preserving the vanishing gating property while allowing heavy-tailed downstream growth.

Third, the per-edge epistemic discount is replaced by an epistemic teleportation vector in PageRank, which mathematically guarantees that spectral random walks restart exclusively in human-verified science, bounding the spectral radius of the AI-generated subgraph regardless of its density.

Fourth, the synthetic control donor pool is restricted to subfields beyond a k-hop network distance from the treated unit, preventing donor contamination through spillover.

The framework incorporates validated alternatives: Embedding Disruptiveness Measure (EDM) instead of the CD index, the Virtual-Geography Hawkes Process for citation dynamics, the TS-Arena pre-registration protocol, and a three-layer validation taxonomy separating derivational validity, representational fidelity, and epistemic significance.

The framework is falsifiable. All quantities are defined rigorously, and the central proposition is stated as a mathematical conjecture with precise falsification conditions.

