1. Introduction

Artificial intelligence is increasingly applied not merely to retrieve scientific knowledge, but to generate mathematical arguments, discover conjectures, construct proofs, formalize results, and explore previously unresolved problems.

The emergence of large AI-generated mathematical corpora creates a new empirical problem. Suppose an AI system produces hundreds of mathematically nontrivial results. How should humanity measure the significance of this event?

A conventional bibliometric approach counts publications, citations, patents, authors, collaborations, or downstream references. These quantities are useful but insufficient. A breakthrough may have enormous importance without immediately accumulating citations. Conversely, a highly cited result may represent incremental progress rather than structural transformation.

This document provides a mathematical specification of a framework for measuring scientific propagation. It defines the objects, states the propositions, provides the proofs, and specifies the algorithms. Empirical validation is deferred to future work.

Scope statement. This is theoretical PhD-level work. No experiments, physical tests, or code training are performed. The contributions are mathematical definitions, propositions, proofs, and algorithmic specifications.

