3. The Gated Cobb–Douglas Impact Function

3.1 Motivation

Version 0.1 defined impact as a linear combination:

I_i(t) = V_i [αQ_i + βN_i + γF_i(t) + δK_i(t) + εX_i(t) + ζT_i(t) + ηE_i(t)]

This formulation suffers from a critical flaw: a result with zero novelty (N_i = 0) or zero quality (Q_i = 0) can still obtain high impact if its connectivity or fertility is high. A well-known result re-derived by AI would receive a high impact score. This is the additive fallacy.

3.2 Definition

Definition 3.1 (Gated Impact Function). For a discovery i with novelty N_i ∈ [0, ∞), quality Q_i ∈ [0, ∞), validation V_i(t) ∈ [0, 1], and propagation vector

P_i(t) = γF_i(t) + δK_i(t) + εX_i(t) + ζT_i(t) + ηE_i(t),

define the impact function

I_i(t) = V_i(t) · N_i^α · Q_i^β · [1 + ln(1 + P_i(t))]

where α, β > 0.

Remark 3.2 (Correction from v0.3). Version 0.3 used the logistic sigmoid σ(P) = 1/(1 + e^{−P}), which satisfies σ(0) = ½ and σ(P) → 1 as P → ∞. This compresses the entire dynamic range of downstream propagation into the narrow factor interval [½, 1), so propagation can at most double the impact, and saturation erases the heavy tail.The corrected multiplier 1 + ln(1 + P) equals 1.0 at baseline (P = 0) and scales unboundedly (with logarithmic sublinear elasticity), preserving vanishing gating while allowing heavy-tailed growth.

3.3 Properties

Proposition 3.3 (Vanishing Property). If N_i = 0 or Q_i = 0, then I_i(t) = 0 for all t and all values of the propagation vector.

Proof. Immediate: N_i^α = 0 or Q_i^β = 0, so the product is zero. □

Proposition 3.4 (Baseline Anchoring). At discovery time t₀, before downstream diffusion occurs (P_i(t₀) = 0), the impact reduces to

I_i(t₀) = V_i(t₀) · N_i^α · Q_i^β.

Proof. ln(1 + 0) = 0, so the multiplier equals 1. □

Proposition 3.5 (Strict Monotonicity in Propagation). For fixed N_i, Q_i, V_i, the function I_i(t) is strictly increasing in P_i(t).

Proof. The derivative of 1 + ln(1 + P) with respect to P is 1/(1 + P) > 0 for all P ≥ 0. □

Proposition 3.6 (Sublinear Growth). For large P, the multiplier grows as ln P, so I_i(t) = O(ln P_i(t)). This preserves heavy-tailed dynamics while preventing quadratic blow-up. □

