4. Mathematical Fertility via Proof-Graph Entropy

4.1 Motivation

Version 0.1 defined fertility as a weighted count of “new research opportunities,” but left the measurement of opportunities unspecified. Version 0.3 introduced proof-graph entropy on the Lean declaration graph, but contained a sign convention error that rendered fertility negative when entropy was reduced.

Version 0.4 corrects this defect by defining mathematical fertility at discovery time t₀ as Information Gain (ΔH) over the proof-search state space.

4.2 Proof-Graph Entropy

Definition 4.1 (Open Goal Reachability Mass). Let 𝒢 ⊆ D_ℒ be a finite set of open target goal states (e.g., benchmark open problems, unproven sorry stubs, or target conjectures in Mathlib). For each goal g ∈ 𝒢, let R(g) ⊆ D_ℒ denote its ancestral reachability set in G_ℒ:

R(g) = {d ∈ D_ℒ : d ⇝ g in G_ℒ}

The normalized goal-mass probability vector p ∈ Δ^{|𝒢|−1} is defined component-wise across 𝒢 by:

p(g) = |R(g)| / Σ_{g' ∈ 𝒢} |R(g')|

Definition 4.2 (Proof-Graph Entropy). The topological entropy of the proof-search state space over open goal set 𝒢 is the Shannon entropy of the reachability distribution p:

H(𝒢) = − Σ_{g ∈ 𝒢} p(g) log₂ p(g)

H(𝒢) quantifies the degree of dispersion in proof-path dependencies. A uniform distribution p(g) = 1/|𝒢| represents maximal entropy (H(𝒢) = log₂|𝒢|), where dependencies are fragmented across independent subfields. A concentrated distribution indicates structural alignment around core foundational modules.

4.3 Formal Fertility as Goal-Mass Information Gain

When a new discovery d_i (a formalized theorem or lemma family) is introduced into D_ℒ, it introduces new directed edges I_ℒ' = I_ℒ ∪ {(u, d_i)}_{u ∈ deps(d_i)}. The updated reachability set for goal g becomes:

R(g | d_i) = R(g) ∪ {d_i} ∪ (⋃_{u ∈ deps(d_i)} R(u))

This updates the probability vector to q = p(· | d_i), yielding the conditional proof-graph entropy H(𝒢 | d_i).

Definition 4.3 (Formal Fertility in v0.4). For a candidate discovery d_i ∈ D_ℒ, its formal fertility at discovery time t₀ is defined as the Information Gain (Entropy Reduction) over the open goal space:

F_i(t₀) = ΔH(𝒢) = H(𝒢) − H(𝒢 | d_i)

Interpretation. A theorem d_i is fertile (F_i(t₀) > 0) if adding it as an available premise bridges previously disjoint proof paths, concentrating the reachability mass onto actionable goal paths and reducing the branching factor required for automated tools to navigate 𝒢.

Remark 4.4 (Correction from v0.3). Version 0.3 defined F = H(𝒢|d_i) − H(𝒢). If a fertile theorem concentrates reachability mass (making the distribution less uniform), then H(𝒢|d_i) < H(𝒢), and F < 0 for highly fertile theorems. The corrected definition F = H(𝒢) − H(𝒢|d_i) yields F > 0 when entropy is reduced. This is the standard information-gain convention.

4.4 Non-Negativity and Goal-Mass Majorization

Proposition 4.5 (Goal-Mass Majorization & Non-Negativity). Let p = p(·) and q = p(· | d_i) be the reachability probability vectors over 𝒢 before and after adding declaration d_i. If introducing d_i preferentially expands reachability for a target subset of open goals 𝒢* ⊂ 𝒢 while leaving the remainder invariant, then q majorizes p (q ≻ p). Consequently, by the Schur-concavity of Shannon entropy:

H(𝒢 | d_i) ≤ H(𝒢) ⟹ F_i(t₀) ≥ 0

4.5 Lean 4 Environment Implementation Details

In Lean 4, formal fertility is operationalized directly via the Lean kernel and metaprogramming environment (Lean.Environment, Lean.Name, Lean.Expr):

Environment Extraction. The project dependencies are built using Lake (lake build). A custom Lean 4 metaprogramming plugin iterates over Lean.Environment.constants to construct G_ℒ.

AST Dependency Parsing. For each declaration d ∈ D_ℒ, its type signature and proof expression (ConstantInfo.value) are traversed to extract all referenced Lean.Expr.const names, defining the directed edges I_ℒ.

Automated Goal Graph Integration. Target goals 𝒢 are represented as open Lean target environments. Automated reasoning tactics (e.g., Lean 4 Aesop, Discretion, or SMT integration scripts) attempt search trees over 𝒢. F_i(t₀) measures the search-depth contraction when d_i is added to the local tactic context (LocalContext).

4.6 Algorithm: Formal Fertility Computation

```
Algorithm 1: Formal Fertility Computation (v0.4)
Input : Lean declaration graph G_L = (D_L, I_L),
        Open goal set G,
        Candidate declaration d_i with dependencies deps(d_i)
Output: Formal fertility score F_i(t_0) >= 0

1.  Initialize reachability map R[g] = {} for each g in G.
2.  For each goal g in G:
        Compute R[g] via reverse BFS on G_L starting from g.
3.  Compute total mass M = sum_{g in G} |R[g]|.
4.  For each goal g in G:
        Compute p(g) = |R[g]| / M.
5.  Compute baseline entropy H_base = - sum_{g in G} p(g) * log2(p(g)).
6.  Construct temporary graph G_L' = (D_L U {d_i}, I_L U {(u, d_i) for u in deps(d_i)}).
7.  For each goal g in G:
        Compute updated reachability R_new[g] via reverse BFS on G_L'.
8.  Compute updated total mass M_new = sum_{g in G} |R_new[g]|.
9.  For each goal g in G:
        Compute q(g) = |R_new[g]| / M_new.
10. Compute posterior entropy H_post = - sum_{g in G} q(g) * log2(q(g)).
11. Return F_i(t_0) = H_base - H_post.
```

Complexity Analysis. Let n = |D_ℒ| ≈ 2.5 × 10⁵ (Mathlib4 scale), m = |I_ℒ| ≈ 1.8 × 10⁶, and k = |𝒢|. Computing single-source reachability via BFS takes O(n + m). For k goals, baseline computation is O(k(n + m)). Because d_i modifies only local forward reachable sets, incremental dynamic graph reachability updates reduce the evaluation step to O(k · deg(d_i)), enabling real-time evaluation of AI-generated proofs.

