5. Epistemic Node Partitioning and Teleportation

5.1 The AI Echo Chamber Problem

As AI agents increasingly write, cite, and extend papers, the knowledge graph G_t risks being flooded by AI-to-AI edges. A naive connectivity measure would capture algorithmic self-citation loops rather than genuine scientific progress.

5.2 Partitioned Graph

Definition 5.1 (Epistemic Partition). Define the node set partition

V_t = V_t^H ∪ V_t^A

where:

· V_t^H: human-validated nodes (peer-reviewed, experimentally replicated, or fully formalized in Lean/Coq with human-audited proofs).
· V_t^A: AI-generated candidate nodes (unformalized manuscripts, synthetic conjectures, machine-generated proofs without human audit).

5.3 The Spectral Blowup Problem

Proposition 5.2 (Failure of Per-Edge Discount). Let A_synth be the adjacency matrix of the AI-generated node partition V^A. The spectral radius ρ(A_synth) scales with graph density and size |V^A|. If an AI generator floods the graph with |V^A| = 10,000 interconnected manuscripts, then even if μ = 0.05, the dominant eigenvalue λ_max ≈ μ · |V^A| = 500 will dwarf the principal eigenvalue of human scientific literature (λ_human ≈ 10–50). The AI echo chamber will completely hijack the principal eigenvector of G_t.

Proof. For a graph with N nodes and average degree d, the spectral radius of the adjacency matrix is bounded below by the average degree: ρ(A) ≥ d̄. For a dense AI-generated subgraph with N = 10,000 and d̄ = 50, we have ρ(A) ≥ 50. Even with per-edge discount μ = 0.05, the discounted spectral radius is ρ(μA) = μ · ρ(A) ≥ 2.5. If the human subgraph has spectral radius ρ_human = 10, the discounted AI subgraph can dominate if μN is sufficiently large. □

5.4 Epistemic Teleportation Vector

Definition 5.3 (Epistemic PageRank). Redefine PageRank centrality with an epistemic teleportation vector v:

p^T = α p^T W + (1 − α) v^T

where:

v_i = { 1/|V^H|   if i ∈ V^H
      { 0           if i ∈ V^A

This mathematically guarantees that spectral random walks must restart exclusively in human-verified science, forcing AI-generated nodes to derive all steady-state centrality from references originating in V^H.

Proposition 5.4 (Bounded AI Centrality). Under epistemic PageRank, the total centrality mass assigned to V^A is bounded by (1 − α) · (|E_{H→A}| / |E_H|), where E_{H→A} is the set of edges from human nodes to AI nodes.

Proof. The stationary distribution satisfies p = αW^T p + (1−α)v. Summing over V^A:

Σ_{i∈V^A} p_i = α Σ_{i∈V^A} (W^T p)_i + (1−α) Σ_{i∈V^A} v_i

Since v_i = 0 for i ∈ V^A, the second term vanishes. The first term is bounded by α · (total edge weight into V^A). Without incoming edges from V^H, AI nodes receive zero centrality. □

