6. Counterfactual Identifiability Under Network Spillover

6.1 The Problem

Suppose scientific output increases after an AI breakthrough. We cannot automatically conclude AI causation. The increase could result from increased funding, new instruments, changes in publication practices, or unrelated discoveries. We must estimate

ΔK(t) = K_observed(t) − K_counterfactual(t).

The standard synthetic control method assumes no interference between units (SUTVA). For scientific fields, this assumption is violated: knowledge diffuses across subfields through the knowledge graph.

6.2 Donor Contamination

Proposition 6.1 (Donor Contamination Bias). If donor subfields u ∈ 𝒟 are themselves affected by spillover from the treated unit u₀, the observed production K_u(t) of donor fields contains a non-zero treatment effect τ_u(t) > 0. Fitting a synthetic control on contaminated donors biases the estimated counterfactual K̂_{u₀}(t) upward, causing ΔK(t) = K_observed − K̂_{u₀} to underestimate true AI impact or produce false negatives.

Proof. Let K_u(t) = K_u^{(0)}(t) + τ_u(t) where K_u^{(0)} is the untreated trajectory and τ_u > 0 is the spillover effect. The synthetic control estimator is K̂_{u₀}(t) = Σ w_u K_u(t) = Σ w_u K_u^{(0)}(t) + Σ w_u τ_u(t). Since Σ w_u τ_u(t) > 0, the estimator overestimates the counterfactual. □

6.3 k-Hop Masked Donor Pool

Definition 6.2 (k-Hop Masked Donor Pool). For a treated subfield u₀, define the donor pool

𝒟^{(k)} = {u ∈ 𝒰 \ {u₀} : d_𝒩(u₀, u) > k}

where d_𝒩(u₀, u) is the shortest path distance in the baseline knowledge graph G_{t₀}.

Theorem 6.3 (Counterfactual Identifiability Under k-Hop Masking). Let 𝒰 be a set of subfields connected by a network 𝒩. Suppose:

1. (Latent factor model) For each subfield u and time t,
   K_u(t) = f_u^T g_t + ε_u(t)
   where f_u ∈ ℝ^r are time-invariant factor loadings, g_t ∈ ℝ^r are common time factors, and ε_u(t) are idiosyncratic errors with E[ε_u(t)] = 0.
2. (Network spillover with finite range) Spillover effects are confined to the k-hop neighborhood of u₀.
3. (Embedding sufficiency) The learned embeddings z_u(t) are sufficient statistics for f_u.

Then the synthetic control estimator K̂_{u₀}(t) using donor pool 𝒟^{(k)} is an unbiased estimator of the counterfactual K_{u₀}^{(0)}(t) for t > t₀.

Proof Sketch. Condition 2 ensures that donors outside the k-hop neighborhood are untreated. Condition 1 ensures that the donor pool spans the factor space. Condition 3 ensures that matching on embeddings is equivalent to matching on factor loadings. Under these conditions, the synthetic control weights converge to weights that reconstruct the treated unit’s factor loadings from the uncontaminated donor pool, yielding unbiased counterfactual estimation. □

Remark 6.4. The embedding-based synthetic control (EB-SC) framework of Zhang et al. (2026) combines nonparametric causal inference with graph neural networks. A graph convolutional autoencoder produces low-dimensional embeddings that encode not only each node’s attributes but also its changing local connectivity, enabling construction of a synthetic node that mimics the treated node’s trajectory in the network.Monte Carlo exercises and a case study of the University of Arizona closure during COVID-19 showed that a network-ignorant comparison underestimated the effect, while EB-SC produced estimates robust to network spillover.

