7. Disruption Measurement: EDM Replaces the CD Index

7.1 The CD Index Failure

Proposition 7.1 (Simultaneous Discovery Blind Spot). The Consolidation–Disruption (CD) Index assigns low disruption scores to simultaneously discovered breakthroughs because it measures whether descendants cite the focal paper’s antecedents. If two teams independently make the same discovery and one cites the other, the CD Index ranks the cited paper as consolidating rather than disruptive.

Evidence. The Higgs mechanism was proposed in 1964 by three independent teams. The CD Index gives the Higgs paper a value in the bottom 0.1% of the distribution because it cited the simultaneous Englert–Brout paper. Approximately 10% of Nobel-linked and APS Milestone papers receive unexpectedly low CD-index values, mostly due to simultaneous discovery.

7.2 Embedding Disruptiveness Measure

Definition 7.2 (EDM). For a paper i, define:

· v_past(i): the embedding of the paper’s cited references, learned via neural language model.
· v_future(i): the embedding of the papers that cite i, learned similarly.

The Embedding Disruptiveness Measure is:

EDM(i) = d(v_past(i), v_future(i))

where d is a distance metric in the embedding space.

Validation. EDM assigns high disruptiveness scores to most landmark contributions across 302 Nobel Prize-winning and 278 milestone papers. The regression analysis reveals that EDM exhibits a much stronger association with landmark status than the CD Index.EDM is stable against small citation changes and identifies simultaneous discoveries that the CD Index misses.

Implementation. A Python package embedding-disruptiveness is available for computing EDM from a citation network.

