8. Citation Dynamics: VG-Hawkes Process

8.1 Self-Exciting Dynamics

Definition 8.1 (Hawkes Process). The citation rate λ_i(t) of discovery i follows a self-exciting point process:

λ_i(t) = μ_i + Σ_{j: t_j < t} α_{ji} · κ(t − t_j)

where μ_i is the baseline rate, α_{ji} is the excitation from prior discovery j, and κ(·) is a memory kernel.

8.2 Virtual-Geography Hawkes Process

Definition 8.2 (VG-Hawkes). The Virtual-Geography Hawkes Process incorporates academic relationships between authors as virtual distances by extending the conventional temporal Hawkes process. The intensity function becomes:

λ_i(t) = μ_i + Σ_{j: t_j < t} α_{ji} · κ(t − t_j) · φ(d_vg(i, j))

where d_vg(i, j) is the virtual geographic distance between authors of papers i and j, and φ is a distance-decay function.

Validation. The VG-Hawkes model consistently achieves higher log-likelihood scores than temporal Hawkes models on real-world datasets and effectively captures citation peaks and distributional patterns.

8.3 Neural Hawkes Process

Definition 8.3 (NHP). The Neural Hawkes Process with continuous-time LSTM (cLSTM) captures the aging effect and the sleeping beauty phenomenon — papers that receive little attention for years before sudden impact — more effectively than parametric models.

