10. Selection Bias as Sensitivity Analysis

10.1 The Truncation Problem

The corpus consists of 722 manuscripts organized into 372 related result families, drawn from an evaluation involving approximately 4,000 problems.The corpus is a filtered output: the catalogue came from applying an unspecified “appropriate level of significance” threshold. Without the denominator, the model cannot separate model quality from selection.

Remark 10.1 (Correction from v0.3). Version 0.3 stated the selection bias adjustment as a proposition. It is not. Without knowing the denominator, the selection cannot be identified. It must be treated as a sensitivity analysis.

10.2 Inverse Mills Ratio Adjustment

If the underlying quality distribution is assumed Gaussian, Q ~ N(μ₀, σ₀²), and the published families represent a right-truncated sample selected via threshold τ = F^{-1}(1 − 372/4000) ≈ μ₀ + 1.48σ₀, then the true systemic impact expectation must be adjusted via the inverse Mills ratio:

E[Q | Q ≥ τ] = μ₀ + σ₀ · φ((τ − μ₀)/σ₀) / (1 − Φ((τ − μ₀)/σ₀))

Caveat. The Gaussian assumption is unvalidated. No source supports the specific claim that quality follows N(μ₀, σ₀²) with a known truncation threshold. This is a modeling assumption, not an empirical finding. The adjustment is a sensitivity analysis, not an identified bound.

Remark 10.2. The Heckman correction and inverse Mills ratio are standard tools for truncation and selection bias in panel data and extreme-value samples.Their application here is exploratory.

