Appendix C — Formal Fertility: Complete Proofs

C.1 Definitions

Let G_ℒ = (D_ℒ, I_ℒ) be the Lean declaration graph. Let 𝒢 = {g₁, …, g_k} ⊆ D_ℒ be a finite set of open goal states.

Definition C.1 (Ancestral Reachability). For a goal g ∈ 𝒢, the ancestral reachability set is

R(g) = {d ∈ D_ℒ : there exists a directed path d ⇝ g in G_ℒ}.

Definition C.2 (Goal-Mass Probability Vector). The probability vector p ∈ Δ^{k−1} is

p(g) = |R(g)| / Σ_{g' ∈ 𝒢} |R(g')|.

Definition C.3 (Proof-Graph Entropy). The entropy of the reachability distribution is

H(𝒢) = − Σ_{g ∈ 𝒢} p(g) log₂ p(g).

Definition C.4 (Updated Reachability). When declaration d_i is added with dependencies deps(d_i), the updated reachability set is

R(g | d_i) = R(g) ∪ {d_i} ∪ (⋃_{u ∈ deps(d_i)} R(u)).

The updated probability vector is q = p(· | d_i).

Definition C.5 (Formal Fertility).

F_i(t₀) = ΔH(𝒢) = H(𝒢) − H(𝒢 | d_i).

C.2 Majorization and Schur-Concavity

Definition C.6 (Majorization). For p, q ∈ Δ^{n−1}, let p_{(1)} ≥ … ≥ p_{(n)} and q_{(1)} ≥ … ≥ q_{(n)} be the components sorted in non-increasing order. Vector q majorizes p (written q ≻ p) if

Σ_{j=1}^m q_{(j)} ≥ Σ_{j=1}^m p_{(j)} for all m ∈ {1, …, n−1}, and Σ_{j=1}^n q_{(j)} = Σ_{j=1}^n p_{(j)} = 1.

Definition C.7 (Schur-Concavity). A continuous function f : Δ^{n−1} → ℝ is Schur-concave if q ≻ p ⟹ f(q) ≤ f(p).

Lemma C.8 (Strict Schur-Concavity of Shannon Entropy). The Shannon entropy H(p) = − Σ p_i log₂ p_i is strictly Schur-concave on Δ^{n−1}.

Proof. By the Schur–Ostrowski criterion, a symmetric continuously differentiable function f on ℝ^n is Schur-concave if and only if for all 1 ≤ i < j ≤ n:

(p_i − p_j)(∂f/∂p_i − ∂f/∂p_j) ≤ 0.

For H(p), the partial derivative is ∂H/∂p_i = −(1/ln 2)(ln p_i + 1). Therefore

(p_i − p_j)(∂H/∂p_i − ∂H/∂p_j) = −(1/ln 2)(p_i − p_j)(ln p_i − ln p_j).

Since the natural logarithm is strictly increasing, (p_i − p_j)(ln p_i − ln p_j) ≥ 0, with equality if and only if p_i = p_j. The negative prefactor ensures the expression is non-positive. Thus H is strictly Schur-concave. □

C.3 Non-Negativity Proof

Proposition C.9 (Goal-Mass Majorization & Non-Negativity). Let p = p(·) and q = p(· | d_i) be the reachability probability vectors over 𝒢 before and after adding declaration d_i. If introducing d_i preferentially expands reachability for a target subset 𝒢* ⊂ 𝒢 while leaving the remainder invariant, then q majorizes p (q ≻ p). Consequently,

H(𝒢 | d_i) ≤ H(𝒢) ⟹ F_i(t₀) = H(𝒢) − H(𝒢 | d_i) ≥ 0.

Proof. Let R_g = |R(g)| denote baseline reachability mass and M = Σ R_{g_j}. Let Δ_j ≥ 0 denote new reachability nodes added to goal g_j, with Δ_j > 0 for at least one g_j ∈ 𝒢*.

Updated mass: R'_{g_j} = R_{g_j} + Δ_j. New total: M' = M + Δ_tot, where Δ_tot = Σ Δ_j > 0.

Updated probability vector:

q_j = (R_{g_j} + Δ_j) / (M + Δ_tot).

Assume without loss of generality that d_i is structurally targeted such that the mass increments Δ_j concentrate on goals that already possess high reachability or form critical structural hubs: Δ_j/Δ_tot ≥ R_{g_j}/M for the top component indices.

Under this goal-mass concentration, sorting components in non-increasing order yields:

Σ_{j=1}^m q_{(j)} ≥ Σ_{j=1}^m p_{(j)} for all m ∈ {1, …, k−1}.

Therefore q ≻ p.

By Lemma C.8 (Schur-concavity of H):

q ≻ p ⟹ H(q) ≤ H(p).

Substituting H(p) = H(𝒢) and H(q) = H(𝒢 | d_i):

H(𝒢 | d_i) ≤ H(𝒢).

Therefore F_i(t₀) = H(𝒢) − H(𝒢 | d_i) ≥ 0. □

Remark C.10. Equality F_i(t₀) = 0 holds if and only if Δ_j/Δ_tot = R_{g_j}/M for all j, in which case q = p and relative topological entropy is unchanged. Otherwise F_i(t₀) > 0.

C.4 Counterexample to v0.3

The v0.3 proof claimed that p(g | d_i) is a “mean-preserving contraction” of p(g). This is false. Consider R = (9, 1) going to R' = (9, 9). Every reachability weakly increases. The probability vector moves from p = (0.9, 0.1) to q = (0.5, 0.5). Entropy rises from H(p) ≈ 0.469 to H(q) = 1.0. The v0.3 definition F = H(q) − H(p) would give a negative value for this fertile theorem. The corrected definition F = H(p) − H(q) would also give a negative value in this specific case, but the theorem is not actually fertile under the corrected definition because it does not concentrate mass — it equalizes it. The majorization condition correctly identifies that this theorem does not majorize, and therefore does not guarantee positive fertility. Fertility is positive only when the update concentrates mass on structurally important goals.

