Appendix E — k-Hop Synthetic Control: Identifiability Proof

E.1 Donor Contamination

Proposition E.1 (Donor Contamination Bias). If donor subfields u ∈ 𝒟 are themselves affected by spillover from the treated unit u₀, the observed production K_u(t) contains a non-zero treatment effect τ_u(t) > 0. Fitting a synthetic control on contaminated donors biases the estimated counterfactual K̂_{u₀}(t) upward, causing ΔK(t) = K_observed − K̂_{u₀} to underestimate true AI impact or produce false negatives.

Proof. Let K_u(t) = K_u^{(0)}(t) + τ_u(t), where K_u^{(0)} is the untreated trajectory and τ_u > 0 is the spillover effect. The synthetic control estimator is

K̂_{u₀}(t) = Σ w_u K_u(t) = Σ w_u K_u^{(0)}(t) + Σ w_u τ_u(t).

Since Σ w_u τ_u(t) > 0, the estimator overestimates the counterfactual. Therefore ΔK = K_observed − K̂_{u₀} underestimates the true effect. □

E.2 k-Hop Masking

Definition E.2 (k-Hop Masked Donor Pool). For a treated subfield u₀:

𝒟^{(k)} = {u ∈ 𝒰 \ {u₀} : d_𝒩(u₀, u) > k},

where d_𝒩(u₀, u) is the shortest path distance in the baseline knowledge graph G_{t₀}.

Theorem E.3 (Counterfactual Identifiability Under k-Hop Masking). Suppose:

1. (Latent factor model) For each subfield u and time t:
   K_u(t) = f_u^T g_t + ε_u(t),
   where f_u ∈ ℝ^r are time-invariant factor loadings, g_t ∈ ℝ^r are common time factors, and ε_u(t) are idiosyncratic errors with E[ε_u(t)] = 0.
2. (Network spillover with finite range) Spillover effects are confined to the k-hop neighborhood of u₀.
3. (Embedding sufficiency) The learned embeddings z_u(t) are sufficient statistics for f_u: E[f_u | z_u(t)] = f_u almost surely.

Then the synthetic control estimator K̂_{u₀}(t) using donor pool 𝒟^{(k)} is an unbiased estimator of the counterfactual K_{u₀}^{(0)}(t) for t > t₀.

Proof Sketch. Condition 2 ensures that donors outside the k-hop neighborhood are untreated: τ_u(t) = 0 for all u ∈ 𝒟^{(k)}. Condition 1 ensures that the donor pool spans the factor space: the factor loadings {f_u}_{u ∈ 𝒟^{(k)}} span ℝ^r. Condition 3 ensures that matching on embeddings is equivalent to matching on factor loadings: E[f_u | z_u] = f_u. Under these conditions, the synthetic control weights w_u converge to weights that reconstruct the treated unit’s factor loadings from the uncontaminated donor pool:

Σ_{u ∈ 𝒟^{(k)}} w_u f_u → f_{u₀}.

Therefore K̂_{u₀}(t) = Σ w_u K_u(t) → f_{u₀}^T g_t = K_{u₀}^{(0)}(t) for t > t₀, yielding unbiased counterfactual estimation. □

Remark E.4. The embedding-based synthetic control (EB-SC) framework of Zhang et al. (2026) combines nonparametric causal inference with graph neural networks. A graph convolutional autoencoder produces low-dimensional embeddings that encode each node’s attributes and changing local connectivity. Monte Carlo exercises and a case study of the University of Arizona closure during COVID-19 showed that a network-ignorant comparison underestimated the effect, while EB-SC produced estimates robust to network spillover.

E.3 Practical Construction of the Donor Pool

For the 2026 case study, the donor pool is constructed as follows:

1. Identify subfields (MSC codes) that received zero or few families from the 372.
2. Compute the subfield network 𝒩 from citation flows, co-authorship, and MSC co-classification.
3. Set k = 2 (two-hop neighborhood). Donors are subfields at distance ≥ 3 from any treated subfield.
4. Validate with placebo tests: apply the method to untreated subfields and check that the estimated ΔK is centered at zero.

