Appendix G — VG-Hawkes Process: Specification

G.1 Standard Hawkes Process

Definition G.1 (Hawkes Process). The citation rate λ_i(t) of discovery i follows

λ_i(t) = μ_i + Σ_{j: t_j < t} α_{ji} · κ(t − t_j),

where μ_i is the baseline rate, α_{ji} is the excitation from prior discovery j, and κ(·) is a memory kernel (typically exponential: κ(s) = β e^{−βs}).

G.2 Virtual-Geography Extension

Definition G.2 (VG-Hawkes). The Virtual-Geography Hawkes Process incorporates academic relationships between authors as virtual distances:

λ_i(t) = μ_i + Σ_{j: t_j < t} α_{ji} · κ(t − t_j) · φ(d_vg(i, j)),

where d_vg(i, j) is the virtual geographic distance between authors of papers i and j, and φ is a distance-decay function (typically φ(d) = e^{−γd}).

G.3 Validation

The VG-Hawkes model consistently achieves higher log-likelihood scores than temporal Hawkes models on real-world datasets and effectively captures citation peaks and distributional patterns. The model is particularly effective for modeling the sleeping beauty phenomenon — papers that receive little attention for years before sudden impact.

G.4 Neural Hawkes Process

Definition G.3 (NHP). The Neural Hawkes Process with continuous-time LSTM (cLSTM) captures the aging effect and the sleeping beauty phenomenon more effectively than parametric models. The intensity function is parameterized by a recurrent neural network with continuous-time dynamics.

