Appendix M — Proof of the Central Proposition

Proposition M.1 (Scientific Breakthrough Propagation). Let 𝒟 be a validated set of AI-generated mathematical discoveries introduced at time t₀ into a knowledge graph G_{t₀}. If the discoveries exhibit sufficiently high novelty, validation, formal fertility, and network connectivity, then their cumulative downstream impact produces a measurable deviation from the counterfactual trajectory:

ΔK(t) = K_𝒟(t) − K₀(t) > 0,

where K_𝒟(t) is the observed production trajectory and K₀(t) is the k-hop masked synthetic control.

Proof Sketch. The impact function I_i(t) is strictly increasing in each of N_i, Q_i, V_i, and P_i(t) (Propositions 3.3–3.5). The formal fertility F_i(t₀) is non-negative under goal-mass concentration (Proposition C.9). The epistemic PageRank centrality K_i(t) is bounded for AI-generated nodes (Proposition D.4), ensuring that connectivity is not contaminated by AI echo chambers. The k-hop masked synthetic control yields an unbiased counterfactual (Theorem E.3), so any positive deviation ΔK(t) > 0 is attributable to the discovery set 𝒟. Therefore, if the discoveries have sufficiently high novelty, validation, fertility, and connectivity, their cumulative impact produces a measurable positive deviation. □

Falsification. The proposition is falsified if ΔK(t) ≤ 0 at all pre-registered horizons T ∈ {1, 3, 5, 10} years, or if the 95% posterior interval for ΔK(t) contains zero.

